course: everyday
lesson: 377
title: Self-defence or just stupid?
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An older boy grabbed Sam's arm by the lockers and squeezed hard, and wouldn't let go. Sam twisted free, ran straight to Mr Hughes on duty and told him everything, shaking. That was self-defence: getting away from someone who is hurting you, and getting help. Sam did not chase the boy or hit him afterwards. He got safe, and that was the whole point.
explain: Jake, on the other hand, told the head he had hit a boy «in self-defence». «What did he do?» asked the head. «He looked at me funny.» Even Jake heard how that sounded. A look is not an attack. Neither is a rude word about your mum — Maddie learned that when she hit a boy for one and still got the same punishment as any other fighter.
explain: Self-defence means only what you need to stop being hurt right now, and then getting away. When Kieran got kicked and then chased the Station Road boy down the street to kick him back, it stopped being defence and became revenge. And when a dog ran at Jake in the park, the bravest thing he did was the dullest: he stood still like a tree, arms by his sides, and shouted for the owner. The dog sniffed and lost interest.
explain: Other cases are harder. Freddie had been called names by Jake for a whole term; one day he snapped and hit Jake. The hit was wrong, but the months of names were wrong first, and Freddie could have told a teacher or his parents instead. When Callum shoved Sam over, Sam stayed down on the floor, didn't fight back, and told a teacher later: not cowardly, because it stopped the fight growing. And when a boy grabbed Maddie's hood and dragged her backwards, she pushed him off to get free and stopped there. That was self-defence.
explain: Sometimes the loudest defence is just your voice. When a boy kept pinching Lily's arm on the bus, she said clearly, «Stop pinching me,» so the whole bus heard. The boy went red and moved seats.
fact: Self-defence means doing only what is needed to stop being hurt right now, then getting away.
fact: Getting free and running to a grown-up is self-defence; chasing someone afterwards is not.
fact: Hitting back after the danger is over is revenge, and schools and the law treat it as fighting.
fact: A look, a rude word or an insult is not a physical attack, so hitting for it is not self-defence.
fact: Saying «stop» loudly so others hear is often the quickest way to make someone stop.
fact: If a dog runs at you, standing still with your arms by your sides and calling for the owner is safest.
fact: Running or waving your arms at a strange dog can make it chase or jump.
fact: Staying down and not fighting back when shoved is not cowardice; it can stop a fight getting bigger.
fact: Someone who is called names for months may snap and hit out, and it is still wrong, but the name-calling was wrong first.
fact: After any attack, telling a trusted adult is part of keeping safe.
exercise: What did Sam do when the older boy squeezed his arm? | Twisted free and ran to a teacher | Punched the boy hard in the face twice | Stood still and cried for a whole hour | Chased the boy all round the school
exercise: Why does Sam's escape count as self-defence? | He only got away and got help, nothing more | He hit the boy much harder | He won the fight in the end | The boy was older than him
exercise: Was Jake's «he looked at me funny» self-defence? | No, a look is not an attack | Yes, a look can hurt you badly | Yes, if the look was very nasty | Only if the boy was much bigger
exercise: Maddie hit a boy for a rude word about her mum. Why wasn't it self-defence? | Words hurt feelings but are not an attack on her body | Because it was about her mum, and that is the worst kind of insult | Because girls can't use self-defence | Because the boy was smaller
exercise: When did Kieran's kick turn into revenge? | When he chased the boy down the street to kick him back | When he was first kicked | When he told his mum about it after school | When he went home
exercise: How did Freddie probably feel before he hit Jake? | Hurt and angry after months of being called names | Bored and looking for fun | Happy and excited | Jealous of Jake's new trainers and his new phone
exercise: Was Freddie right to hit Jake? | No, but Jake's months of names were wrong too | Yes, because Jake deserved everything he got after all those months | Yes, because he waited so long | No, and Jake did nothing wrong
exercise: What could Freddie have done instead of hitting? | Told a teacher or parent about the name-calling | Called Jake worse names | Waited for the right moment and hit him harder later on | Got his friends to hit Jake
exercise: Sam was shoved over and stayed on the floor. Was that cowardly? | No, it stopped a fight and he could tell a teacher | Yes, he should have fought back | Yes, boys must always fight back | No, because he was asleep
exercise: Why did Jake stand still when the dog ran at him? | Running or waving can make a dog chase or jump | He was too scared to move his legs, so he just froze there | He wanted to stroke it | He was waiting to kick it
exercise: What did Lily do about the boy pinching her on the bus? | Said «stop pinching me» loudly so everyone heard | Pinched him back twice as hard so he would know what it felt like | Said nothing and let it go on | Got off the bus at the wrong stop
exercise: Why did Lily's loud words work? | The boy was embarrassed when everyone knew | Loud words hurt his ears | The driver threw him off the bus at the very next stop | Lily is stronger than him
exercise: Maddie pushed away a boy who grabbed her hood and dragged her backwards. Was that self-defence? | Yes, she only pushed him off to get free | No, pushing is always fighting | No, she should have let him drag her along to be safe | Yes, and she could hit him after too
exercise: Which is the wisest thing to do after you get away from someone hurting you? | Tell a trusted adult what happened | Plan with your friends how to get them back | Keep it secret so no one worries about it | Post all about it online for everyone
exercise: Which kind of courage did Lily show on the bus? | Speaking up out loud in front of everyone | Fighting a bigger boy on the way home from school | Running away from the bus | Ignoring it for weeks
exercise: Which kind of courage did Jake show with the dog? | Keeping still and calm when every bit of him wanted to run | Fighting the dog off with his school bag and his feet | Laughing at the owner | Chasing the dog away
test: «he looked at me dead funny so i decked him, self-defence innit». Is it? | No, a look isn't an attack | Yes, a dirty look counts as one | Yes, if he looked at you twice, it's an attack | Only if he's older than you
test: «Kieran got kicked so he ran after the lad and booted him back, fair's fair». Is it self-defence? | No, chasing someone to hit back is revenge | Yes, he was kicked first, so he's allowed to kick back | Yes, it's only fair | Only if he kicked softer
test: «some kid grabbed my arm and wouldn't let go so i yanked free and legged it to sir». Good move? | Yes, getting free and getting help is self-defence | No, you should have hit him first | No, you should have stayed there and argued it out with him | Yes, but only if you hit him too
test: «big dog coming at us, RUN!!» What's the safer thing to do? | Stand still, arms down, call for the owner | Run away as fast as you can and don't look back | Wave your arms and scream at it | Throw your bag at it
test: «Freddie finally lamped Jake after like a whole term of names, Jake's well shocked». Which is true? | Hitting was wrong, and so was the long name-calling | Jake got exactly what he asked for | Freddie did nothing wrong at all, he was only defending himself | Only Freddie was ever in the wrong
test: «Sam just lay there when Callum shoved him lol what a wimp». Fair? | No, staying down stopped the fight getting worse | Yes, he should have got up swinging at the bigger boy | Yes, lying down is giving up | No, because Sam was tired
test: Dare: «go on, if he says your mum's fat you HAVE to hit him, it's self-defence». True? | No, rude words aren't an attack on your body | Yes, mum jokes count as attacks, so you can hit back | Yes, but only one punch | Only if it's a really bad joke
test: Teacher: «Lily, why didn't you just pinch him back?» Best answer? | Saying it loudly stopped him without starting a fight | I was too weak to pinch | I forgot I could | Pinching is only for babies, so I just ignored it all day
test: «she pushed him off when he grabbed her hood, she's gonna get excluded». Will she, fairly? | She shouldn't be, she only pushed him off to get free | Yes, all pushing gets you excluded, whoever started it | Yes, girls can't push boys | Only if he cried
test: «once ur safe ya don't tell anyone, snitches get stitches». What's wise? | Tell a trusted adult, it's part of staying safe | Keep it secret forever so nobody worries about you | Only tell your mates | Post it online instead
test: «real self-defence is hitting first before they can hit you». Is that right? | No, hitting first is starting a fight | Yes, always strike first so the other person can't hit you | Yes, if you're scared, always strike first | Only if they're bigger and older than you
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the notion of self-defence as applied to pupils? | Doing only what is necessary to cease being harmed, followed by withdrawal and reporting | Retaliating with considerably greater force than was originally received by the pupil | Responding physically to any verbal provocation | Pursuing the aggressor so as to deter future attacks
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the pursuit of an assailant subsequent to an attack does not constitute self-defence. | The danger has ceased, whereupon the act becomes retaliation | The pursuer is invariably smaller than the assailant and therefore requires protection | Pursuit is permitted only on school premises | The assailant must first be warned in writing
exam: Determine the conduct most consistent with personal safety in respect of an unrestrained dog advancing upon a child. | Remaining motionless with arms lowered and summoning the owner | Fleeing at the greatest possible speed | Waving the arms vigorously and shouting at the animal | Advancing steadily towards the dog in order to intimidate it and drive it off
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning insults directed at a pupil's family member. | They do not constitute a physical attack and do not justify striking another | They constitute an assault upon the person which warrants an immediate physical response | They justify a single blow but no more | They are permissible only when uttered in jest
